comment:
Nice tests! Can we also have a test where we invoke `inOrder` with a different class? E.g. I would expect the following use case to be illegal:

```java
// Declaring `Bar` here rather than `Foo`
InOrder inOrder = inOrder(Bar.class);

mocked.when(Foo::firstMethod).thenReturn("first");
mocked.when(Foo::secondMethod).thenReturn("second");

assertEquals("first", Foo.firstMethod());
assertEquals("second", Foo.secondMethod());

// Execution verification on `Foo`, while `inOrder` was created with `Bar`
inOrder.verify(mocked, Foo::firstMethod, times(1));
inOrder.verify(mocked, Foo::secondMethod, atLeastOnce());
```

I think it would be better to address this in one place by changing `getSuiteDisplayName` above to the following:

```java
private static String getSuiteDisplayName(Class<?> suiteClass, DiscoveryIssueReporter issueReporter) {
	var nonBlank = issueReporter.createReportingCondition(StringUtils::isNotBlank, __ -> {
		String message = "@SuiteDisplayName on %s must be declared with a non-blank value.".formatted(
			suiteClass.getName());
		return DiscoveryIssue.builder(Severity.WARNING, message) //
				.source(ClassSource.from(suiteClass)) //
				.build();
	}).toPredicate();
	return findAnnotation(suiteClass, SuiteDisplayName.class) //
			.map(SuiteDisplayName::value) //
			.filter(nonBlank) //
			.orElse(suiteClass.getSimpleName());
}
```

Because this is tied to `isSuccessful` I would expect optional-like semantics.

Something like 

```
result.toException().ifPresent(exception -> {
    LOGGER.warn(exception, exception::getMessage);
});
```

Can we simplify this by removing the outer condition? We are checking the same startsWith conditions again inside it.


```suggestion
String currentType = type;
if (currentType.startsWith(WILDCARD_EXTENDS_PREFIX)) { 
currentType = currentType.substring(WILDCARD_EXTENDS_PREFIX.length()); 
} else if (currentType.startsWith(WILDCARD_SUPER_PREFIX)) { currentType = currentType.substring(WILDCARD_SUPER_PREFIX.length()); 
} else {
 currentType = stripTrailingParameterName(currentType); 
 }
```
